WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:08.664
Dual Raster draws a second, independent raster alongside the first, like the two-channel scan processors of the seventies.

2
00:00:08.664 --> 00:00:10.492
Here the source is a camera.

3
00:00:10.948 --> 00:00:14.105
Open the Dual Raster section and switch on raster B.

4
00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:16.879
Trace splits the frame between the two.

5
00:00:16.879 --> 00:00:19.665
Sweep it across: A on one side, B on the other.

6
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.515
Raster B has its own geometry.

7
00:00:26.515 --> 00:00:29.104
Push its depth the other way, and turn it.

8
00:00:33.584 --> 00:00:36.254
Alternate lines interleaves them instead.

9
00:00:36.254 --> 00:00:38.533
Every other scan line belongs to B.

10
00:00:38.988 --> 00:00:40.543
B still shows the camera.

11
00:00:40.543 --> 00:00:47.137
In the Patch Bay, give it a picture of its own: the Pattern Generator's output into Dual Raster's B input.

12
00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.235
Then the Dual Raster's output into the Renderer.

13
00:00:52.973 --> 00:00:54.133
Restore the window.

14
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.922
The camera and the pattern now share one raster, line by line.

15
00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:03.813
Each raster can take a camera, a file, a stream, a pattern, or a composite of two.

16
00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:04.608
Next, sound.
